.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-2-Clause
.Dd October 11, 2026
.Dt LIBSYSCTLCMP 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm sysctlcmp_client_open ,
.Nm sysctlcmp_client_create ,
.Nm sysctlcmp_client_close ,
.Nm sysctlcmp_get ,
.Nm sysctlcmp_set ,
.Nm sysctlcmp_oidfmt ,
.Nm sysctlcmp_describe ,
.Nm sysctlcmp_next ,
.Nm sysctlcmp_interfaces
.Nd client interface to the system.Sysctl capability
.Sh LIBRARY
Sysctl Capability Library (libsysctlcmp, -lsysctlcmp)
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sysctlcmp.h
.Ft int
.Fn sysctlcmp_client_open "struct sysctlcmp_client **clientp"
.Ft int
.Fn sysctlcmp_client_create "int fd" "struct sysctlcmp_client **clientp"
.Ft void
.Fn sysctlcmp_client_close "struct sysctlcmp_client *client"
.Ft int
.Fn sysctlcmp_get "struct sysctlcmp_client *client" "const char *name" "void *buf" "size_t *lenp"
.Ft int
.Fn sysctlcmp_set "struct sysctlcmp_client *client" "const char *name" "const void *value" "size_t len"
.Ft int
.Fn sysctlcmp_oidfmt "struct sysctlcmp_client *client" "const char *name" "unsigned int *kindp" "char *fmt" "size_t *fmtlenp"
.Ft int
.Fn sysctlcmp_describe "struct sysctlcmp_client *client" "const char *name" "char *buf" "size_t *lenp"
.Ft int
.Fn sysctlcmp_next "struct sysctlcmp_client *client" "const char *name" "char *buf" "size_t *lenp"
.Ft int
.Fn sysctlcmp_interfaces "struct sysctlcmp_client *client" "struct sysctlcmp_interface *out" "size_t *count"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
These functions read and write kernel
.Xr sysctl 3
variables by name through the
.Dq system.Sysctl
capability rather than by calling
.Xr sysctl 3
directly, so a capability-mode program obtains sysctl access as a held
capability, subject to the provider's per-label policy.
.Pp
.Fn sysctlcmp_client_open
acquires the capability over the process's lookup channel.
.Fn sysctlcmp_get
reads
.Fa name
into
.Fa buf ;
.Fa *lenp
is the buffer size on entry and the value length on return.
If the buffer is too small the call fails with
.Er ENOMEM
and
.Fa *lenp
holds the required size.
.Fn sysctlcmp_set
writes
.Fa value
to
.Fa name ,
which the provider permits only when the caller's label is authorized to write
that variable.
.Pp
.Fn sysctlcmp_oidfmt
returns the sysctl kind in
.Fa *kindp
and its NUL-terminated format string in
.Fa fmt .
.Fn sysctlcmp_describe
returns the NUL-terminated description of
.Fa name .
For both calls, the associated length argument is the buffer size on entry and
receives the returned size, including the terminating NUL.
.Fn sysctlcmp_next
returns the next policy-visible sysctl name after
.Fa name ;
an empty or NULL name starts enumeration and
.Er ENOENT
marks its end.
The returned name is NUL-terminated.
Malformed string replies fail with
.Er EPROTO
and invalidate the session.
.Pp
.Fn sysctlcmp_client_create
adopts an already held system.Sysctl channel
.Fa fd ;
it consumes the descriptor only on success.
The library speaks the FIDL protocol
.Sy bsd.sysctl.v1
.Pq Pa /usr/share/fidl/sysctlcmp.fidl ;
see
.Xr fidl 7 .
The first call on a client checks the provider's wire version and fails with
.Er EPROTONOSUPPORT
for an incompatible provider.
Each call is bounded by five seconds, shortened by an active
.Fn service_budget_begin
budget
.Pq Xr service_runtime 3 .
A timeout fails that call with
.Er ETIMEDOUT
and cancels it; the session stays usable.
A transport failure or malformed reply makes the client terminal:
later calls fail with the same error until it is closed.
Application errors keep it usable.
Calls from several threads on one client are serialized.
.In sysctlcmp_server.h
and the message records in
.In sysctlcmp_protocol.h
describe the pre-FIDL wire format; they are kept for compatibility only.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Rv -std sysctlcmp_client_open sysctlcmp_client_create sysctlcmp_get sysctlcmp_set sysctlcmp_oidfmt sysctlcmp_describe sysctlcmp_next
.Sh INTERFACE INVENTORY
.Fn sysctlcmp_interfaces
reads a bounded interface inventory through the provider's fixed read-only
kernel query.
It requires read permission for
.Dq net.routetable
under the caller's authenticated label.
It accepts no numeric MIB, interface selector, or write data.
This operation does not change the provider's protected-OID isolation set.
.Pp
Each
.Vt struct sysctlcmp_interface
contains the index, native interface and driver flags, MTU, link state, and a
zero-padded NUL-terminated name.
Link state is
.Dv SYSCTLCMP_LINK_UNKNOWN ,
.Dv SYSCTLCMP_LINK_DOWN ,
or
.Dv SYSCTLCMP_LINK_UP ;
it does not indicate Internet connectivity.
Addresses, hardware addresses, and traffic counters are omitted.
Records are sorted by index, with unique indexes and names.
The kernel walk is not a transaction across all interfaces.
.Pp
On entry,
.Fa count
holds the output capacity in records; on success it holds the number returned.
Passing NULL output with zero capacity queries the current count.
A short non-NULL output fails with
.Er ENOMEM
and sets the required count without copying any records.
Other failures leave the output and count unchanged.
A later call takes a fresh snapshot, so its count may differ.
.Pp
The limit is 256 interfaces and a four-megabyte native kernel dump;
exceeding either fails with
.Er EOVERFLOW .
Repeated growth across three collection attempts fails with
.Er EAGAIN .
Malformed responses fail with
.Er EPROTO .
There is no fallback to another request; no fallback widens authority.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr service_runtime 3 ,
.Xr sysctl 3 ,
.Xr fidl 7 ,
.Xr BSDSysctl 8
.Sh SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
Read and write access is scoped per label by the provider; a variable not
named in the caller's allow-list is refused with
.Er EPERM ,
and writes are denied by default.
